<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shrinathji</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shrinathji is a form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> depicted as a seven-year-old child lifting Govardhan Hill, worshipped as the principal deity of the Pushtimarg tradition founded by Vallabhacharya. The image, said to have been found on Govardhan Hill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, was moved in 1672 to Mewar to escape Mughal hostility and installed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathdwara">Nathdwara</a>, which became one of the major pilgrimage centres of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shrinathji.jpg" alt="Shrinathji" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nathdwara Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shrinathji_Vitthalanath_Birthday_Shringar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to legend the icon manifested itself from stone and emerged from Govardhan Hill, where it was first worshipped. The child deity was at first called Devadāman, the conqueror of gods, recalling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> in lifting the hill; Vallabhacharya named him Gopala and his place of worship Gopalpur, and Vallabhacharya's son Vitthal Nathji gave him the name Shrinathji and institutionalised his worship.</p><p>On leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> the image was kept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> for almost six months, to protect it, as tradition holds, from the Mughal ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, who wished to destroy it. It was then taken south on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, and at the village of Sihad in Mewar the wheels are said to have sunk into the mud, which the priests took as the deity's choice of place. It was installed there under the protection of Maharana Raj Singh of Mewar. In the late 18th and early 19th centuries, when the temple was attacked by the Medas and the Pindaris, the icon was moved again to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> and Ghasiyar under Maharana Bheem Singh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The icon is carved in bas-relief from a single black marble stone, with two cows, a lion, a snake, two peacocks and a parrot engraved on it and three sages beside it. It wears jewels, some dating from before the Mughal period, and its clothes are changed eight times a day and never worn again but given to devotees. Its temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathdwara">Nathdwara</a>, 48 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, was built in the 17th century and is called Shrinathji ki <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haveli">Haveli</a>, the house of Shrinathji, with store rooms, a kitchen, a treasury and a stable like a mansion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Devotees treat the deity as a living child who is bathed, dressed, fed and rested. Following a legend of Yashoda guarding her son from his admirers, Vallabhacharya opened the inner sanctum at eight set times a day, from Mangala at dawn to Shayan after the evening meal. The priests are held to be descendants of Vallabhacharya. Over 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathdwara">Nathdwara</a> in a year, many at Janmashtami, Holi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>, and the pichhwai paintings on cloth that portray Shrinathji are made there. Shrinathji temples also stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, where one was built in 1817, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shrinathji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1672 the image of <b>Shrinathji</b>, a form of Krishna, was brought to Nathdwara in Mewar, which became a major pilgrimage centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
